WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.124 --> 00:00:04.972
Marcus Bell: Hey Dana, how's it going?

2
00:00:05.602 --> 00:00:13.008
Dana Ortiz: Good, good. Busy week. We're finally past the shoulder season rush, so I can actually breathe for a minute.

3
00:00:13.984 --> 00:00:28.019
Marcus Bell: That's the window I was hoping to catch you in. Thanks for making time. I know the renewal's on the calendar for January, so I figured we'd do a real working session now rather than scramble in December.

4
00:00:28.798 --> 00:00:34.772
Dana Ortiz: Yeah, that's smart. December is a mess for us anyway with the holiday scheduling across the branches.

5
00:00:35.282 --> 00:00:43.307
Marcus Bell: Before we get into the renewal piece, how are things running? Call volumes holding up across the fourteen branches?

6
00:00:43.639 --> 00:00:53.350
Dana Ortiz: They are. We're right around 21,000 calls a month now, maybe a little more. Charlotte and Raleigh are carrying most of it, Greensboro's been creeping up too.

7
00:00:54.336 --> 00:01:00.545
Marcus Bell: That's up from what I had, which was closer to 19,000 in the spring. Nice.

8
00:01:01.625 --> 00:01:07.551
Dana Ortiz: It's the electrical side, mostly. We added two trucks in Raleigh in August and the call flow followed.

9
00:01:08.692 --> 00:01:15.666
Marcus Bell: How's the staffing on your end holding up with that? I know you had somebody out for a while.

10
00:01:16.778 --> 00:01:28.662
Dana Ortiz: We did. Renee was out on leave through the summer, she's back now, which helps a lot. She's the one who actually lives in the platform day to day.

11
00:01:28.928 --> 00:01:40.077
Marcus Bell: Good, glad she's back. So the Google Ads piece, are the offline conversions still landing the way you expect? That's the thing I want to make sure isn't quietly broken before we talk numbers.

12
00:01:40.965 --> 00:01:49.806
Dana Ortiz: As far as I know it's fine. Renee checks the match rates weekly and she hasn't flagged anything to me.

13
00:01:51.186 --> 00:01:56.942
Marcus Bell: Great. And PreSense, now that it's been in since the spring, any operational headaches?

14
00:01:57.926 --> 00:02:05.182
Dana Ortiz: No, it's been quiet. The branches that have it like seeing where the caller came from before they pick up.

15
00:02:06.559 --> 00:02:12.496
Marcus Bell: Okay, that's what I wanted to hear. So let's talk about the renewal itself. Where's your head at?

16
00:02:13.561 --> 00:02:19.917
Dana Ortiz: Honestly, the renewal is fine in principle. Nobody's looking to leave. The problem is procurement.

17
00:02:20.822 --> 00:02:22.041
Marcus Bell: Okay, say more.

18
00:02:22.397 --> 00:02:38.081
Dana Ortiz: Finance and procurement are pushing back hard on the way the add-ons are billed. They see Call Tracking on one line, then PreSense on another line, and then they start asking why the seats and the minutes are all separate.

19
00:02:39.327 --> 00:02:42.340
Marcus Bell: So it's the structure more than the total?

20
00:02:43.295 --> 00:02:53.663
Dana Ortiz: It's the structure. They keep saying they don't want to pay for three or four separate things that all show up under the same vendor.

21
00:02:53.663 --> 00:03:00.714
Dana Ortiz: What they want is one bundled price for everything we use, one number, one line item, done.

22
00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:05.565
Marcus Bell: Got it. And is this coming from a specific person over there?

23
00:03:06.166 --> 00:03:22.411
Dana Ortiz: It's mostly Victor in procurement. He owns the vendor review, and he's the one who flagged it. He's not wrong that it's confusing to read, but he's also not the one who has to make the platform work.

24
00:03:22.921 --> 00:03:26.259
Marcus Bell: Sure. When does he need something in hand?

25
00:03:26.804 --> 00:03:41.035
Dana Ortiz: He wants the renewal package to be one consolidated number. If it comes through with separate line items for the add-ons again, he's going to kick it back and we'll be doing this in January instead of now.

26
00:03:41.303 --> 00:03:51.596
Marcus Bell: That's helpful, and I'd rather know that today than in December. Let me take that back to our team and see what's possible on the packaging side.

27
00:03:51.596 --> 00:03:55.027
Marcus Bell: I can't promise a specific structure on this call.

28
00:03:55.817 --> 00:04:00.331
Dana Ortiz: I get that. I just need something to put in front of him.

29
00:04:01.143 --> 00:04:05.647
Marcus Bell: You'll have it. Who else weighs in on the final sign-off besides Victor?

30
00:04:06.042 --> 00:04:15.324
Dana Ortiz: Me, our VP of operations, and then it goes to finance for the actual approval. I'm the one who has to defend it, so the cleaner the better.

31
00:04:16.026 --> 00:04:19.573
Marcus Bell: Understood. Anything else on the renewal that's rattling around?

32
00:04:20.955 --> 00:04:31.034
Dana Ortiz: That's really it. The rest is just logistics. Oh, one thing, can we push our next quarterly review to the first week of November?

33
00:04:31.034 --> 00:04:36.494
Dana Ortiz: The last week of October is our fall promotion and I'll be buried.

34
00:04:36.779 --> 00:04:46.444
Marcus Bell: First week of November works. I'll send an invite for the 4th at ten and you can move it if that's bad.

35
00:04:47.254 --> 00:04:54.207
Dana Ortiz: Perfect. And I should probably get Renee on that one too, she'll have better questions than I will.

36
00:04:54.698 --> 00:05:04.529
Marcus Bell: Please do. So my follow-up is the packaging question, I'll go dig into it internally and come back to you with something concrete, probably within a week or so.

37
00:05:04.529 --> 00:05:05.207
Marcus Bell: Sound right?

38
00:05:06.539 --> 00:05:08.192
Dana Ortiz: Sounds right. Thanks Marcus.

39
00:05:08.698 --> 00:05:10.015
Marcus Bell: Thanks Dana, talk soon.
